=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault does not exist in the live lab. The helpdesk ticket claims router16 "is down" and that the Campus 1 edge router has lost its OSPF neighbors, cutting off the 172.16.10/20/30.0/24 subnets — but direct inspection refutes every part of that claim.

**Router16** is fully operational: its bonded uplink (eth0+eth1 → bond0, 10.2.2.1/24) to mswitch0 is UP, its LAN link to router7 (eth2, 172.30.1.1/24) is UP, and FRRouting/ospfd is running. Its OSPF neighbour table shows two Full adjacencies — mswitch0 (neighbour-ID 172.16.30.254, address 10.2.2.2, Full/Backup) and router7 (neighbour-ID 192.168.10.1, 172.30.1.2, Full/DR) — both with healthy Dead Timers well above zero. The routing table on router16 carries a complete set of OSPF-learned prefixes, including the Campus 1 subnets (172.16.10–30/24) learned via mswitch0, and all remote networks learned via router7.

**mswitch0** (the Campus 1 multilayer switch) is equally healthy: FRR/ospfd is running, it holds a Full/DR adjacency back to router16, and its kernel routing table shows OSPF routes to all remote networks via 10.2.2.1. Its VLAN interfaces (vlan10 172.16.10.254, vlan20 172.16.20.254, vlan30 172.16.30.254) are up, and Campus 1 end-hosts use these as default gateways.

**End-to-end reachability** from all three Campus 1 VLANs was confirmed: pc5 (VLAN 10), pc9 (VLAN 20), and pc12 (VLAN 30) each pinged router7 with 0% packet loss across the router16 → router7 forwarding path.

Root cause: no fault is present. The ticket is based on a false premise; router16 and all Campus 1 paths are operating normally. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===